From b39b0e3457a51e5d6b934a13a8bde2d7aec30876 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 10:55:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2040/2064] VideoLayerBridgeDRMPRIME: count buffers released
 before their replacement flips

The direct-to-plane bridge keeps exactly two references to the buffers it has
handed to KMS - m_buffer and m_prev_buffer - and frees on a frame count.
Acquire() releases m_prev_buffer on the assumption that the commit presenting
m_buffer in its place has already flipped, which holds for a one frame deep
present pipeline where each commit lands before the next frame is rendered.

The GUI present is now two frames deep. The CPU is paced on the previous flip in
FencePostSwap, which runs after the render, so the Acquire() for a frame - which
happens during that render - can run while the previous commit is still pending.
The buffer released there is then still being scanned out. Nothing else holds
it: the render manager frees every buffer but m_iLastRenderBuffer, which is why
the bridge keeps its own reference at all. It goes back to the decoder pool, the
decoder refills it with a picture from as far ahead as it is working, and the
plane scans that out until the next flip.

Stereoscopic playback is where that is expected to show, because it is where the
spare pipeline slot gets occupied: the GUI layer is rendered once per eye, and
frame packing composites 1920x2205 rather than 1920x1080, so the render is long
enough to still be running when a 2D one would have finished before the flip.

Measure it rather than argue about it. Give each atomic commit a sequence number
and keep a dup of its out-fence - the commit's own fd belongs to the EGL fence
path, which takes it - so GetCommitState() can report whether a given commit has
flipped, by testing the fence with a zero timeout poll() rather than waiting on
it. The sequence counts every commit issued, including those that carry no
out-fence and those that fail, so a sequence recorded earlier still names its
own commit.

Acquire() records the sequence of the commit that will present the buffer it is
taking on, and CheckScanoutRelease() asks about it immediately before either of
the two places that let go of m_prev_buffer. A commit still pending there is
counted and logged: once as it happens, then summarised every five seconds,
since at a display rate this can be every frame.

A commit that carried no out-fence - a video layer only commit, or a legacy non
atomic one - is counted separately as indeterminate, because a zero early count
means nothing unless the indeterminate count is low as well.

The cost is one poll() per released buffer, on the presenting thread. That is
also the thread issuing the commits, so the sequence needs no synchronisation.

(cherry picked from commit a64bd848c45da85df6a8c586b372e724023ab9f7)
